> One of the reasons R is so slow and wasteful of memory is the
> generic data types.

I'm afraid that there is an inescapable trade-of between generality (expressiveness) and efficiency. Lisp's list is possibly the more general representation possible, but managing it *efficiently* is a nightmare (see for example the problems of implementing an efficient garbage collector...).

R gives us a good illustration of this trade-of. For common use; S3-type objects, which is basically a set of naming conventions, is extremely simple to use and extend. Perfect for prototyping. When one want to develop a library (infrastructure), S4-type objects, much more heavy to use, offer serious advantage. If you need speed, you have to go to C/C++ (with Rcpp offering a nice bridge), but you lose all the conveniences of Lisp, and have to manage yourself a lot of details...

>
> > Saint John MacCarthy, ora pro nobis...
>
> You could always try the Church sampler.  It's basically
> Scheme.

I've tried it. As far as I know, it is no longer developed...

