On 10/3/13 12:57 PM, Sergio Polini wrote:
> Yes, but Bob is right.
> English is the lingua franca in computer science.

That wasn't what I was saying!  Now I wish I could turn comments
off on this thread.

I've thought about it a bit more and non-ASCII comments
are OK, as long as the char encoding encodes ASCII as ASCII
(i.e., the code points below 128 match ASCII and ASCII
is encoded as bytes).  This includes ASCII, Latin1,
other Latin-X, Win-1252, and the UTF-8 encoding of Unicode.
It does not include Big5, UTF-16 (either BE or LE versions),
etc.  For instance, UTF-16 matches ASCII on code points, but
uses 16-bit output padded for ASCII.

- Bob

> PS: A(n even) better sampling engine would be much better than UTF-8 comments :-)

That's a bit more work!  But Michael's working on
the actual sampler and working with Peter Li on the model encodings.
Look forward to some great HMC papers over the next few months.

