I think the effect is supposed to be pervasive.  For source packages,
it means text files like changes, README, licenses are in UTF-8.  So
contributors with names that have non-ASCII characters can put their
names in those files.  For the terminal it means accepting and
rendering non-ASCII.

As you observe, there are a lot of things that need to change; it's
possible some of them haven't yet.  And most of the tools are capable
of dealing with and producing different encodings.

I haven't tried giving g++ a file with non-ASCII comments; I hope it
would work :)

Ross

