  http://www.ats.ucla.edu/stat/r/library/contrast_coding.htm

I'm not sure.  But it's definitely in the ballpark.

>> I'm probably not asking this the right way, but I hope people
>> know what I mean.
>
> I'm not sure I follow, in particular about why it matters if the
> scales are smaller or larger.
>
> I think you are comparing estimates for A in 2 models:
> I) y ~ A + B  (R notation for y as a function of 2 main effects)
> II) y ~ A + B + A:B  (A:B is the interaction).
>
> and then you are asking about the magnitude of the effects estimated
> for the A term in I and II.

Thanks --- that's the right way to ask the question :-)

I think it would matter in setting priors for A, B, and A:B.

You also have be careful in interpreting the effect of changes
in A, because they are changes in A and A:B.  But that I know
how to do.  And it may be what contr.sum is doing.

- Bob

