I've never seen a Dirichlet regression like this, so take the
following with a grain of salt.

The code looks OK -- Stan 2 has some simpler vector slicing functions
that'll let you clean up the loops.

My guess is that if the coefficients get large, then

    alphavec <- exp(xm * coef)

is going to cause an overflow --- it pretty much has to be.

With the Dirichlet, you get the mean and "precision" (i.e., prior
count) rolled together.  It might help to estimate them separately.
Maybe something like a multi-logit for the precision and then letting
the scale be estimated separately from the actual error.  Something like:

   parameters {
     vector[C] coef;
     real<lower=0> prior_count;
   }
   model {
     vector<lower=0>[N] alphavec;
     alphavec <- prior_count * softmax(xm * coef);
     prior_count ~ exp(1);
     ...
   }

I'm not sure what a good prior would be on the prior_count.
Andrew argued for a Pareto in the beta (N=2) case in chapter 5
of BDA 2 (unchanged in BDA3 as far as I know), but he told me
personally he doesn't like that prior.

- Bob

