I thought this error meant the program was unable to evaluate the
probability or its derivatives at the starting values.

But below shows it can evaluate the gradients at the first point it
tried (I presume it's the same as the first point in the 100 attempts).
And since it evaluates numeric derivatives, that means it can evaluate
the (log?) probability at least very close to the original point.

Maybe my result list got cut off with an NA?  92 beta + 335 alpha + 4
sigma = 431, and 431 gradients came back.  There are additional
transformed parameters, but I'd guess we don't get their gradients. So
they seem to be complete.

Ross

