I'm not going to be able to stay up much later so I won't be of much help until the morning.

>From what I've gathered over the last few hours, the adaptation appears to be working as it should, with the possible
exception of the estimation of the variances.  The HUGE variations in scale are very much stressing the adaptation.

One diagnostic that someone can run -- compare the epsilon * M^{-1}_{ii} for 1.3 and 2.0 (despite the refactoring,
that product will be comparable).  For any values of the product that are wildly different take a look at the trace of the
respective variable  (given the covariance types you'll have to use the diagnostic output option).

My best guess is that the significant change in parameter values during the rapid warmup (iterations 0-20 or so) is
biasing the initial variance estimates, which then biases the next round of samples, biasing the next variance estimate,
and so on.  Because the algorithm factored out the common scale it might have been more robust to this particular
pathology.  Hacking the adaptation to NOT update the variances during the first adaptation window would help if
this is the case (add a if (_adapt_var_counter > 10) to line 58 of var_adapter.hpp).

