> The traceplots in 2.0 look OK, just slow to mix.  They're converging
> and getting the right answer, it's just that the step size winds up
> way too small in the end.  At least I think it's way too small.

But that's the thing -- the small step sizes yield a reasonable accept stat distribution with the desired average.  The step size
adaptation behaves as expected -- either the integrator error is much larger (possible floating point precision is a factor here
with the large variations in scale and high dimension?) or the variance estimates are way off.

>> My best guess is that the significant change in parameter values during the rapid warmup (iterations 0-20 or so) is
>> biasing the initial variance estimates, which then biases the next round of samples, biasing the next variance estimate,
>> and so on.  Because the algorithm factored out the common scale it might have been more robust to this particular
>> pathology.  Hacking the adaptation to NOT update the variances during the first adaptation window would help if
>> this is the case (add a if (_adapt_var_counter > 10) to line 58 of var_adapter.hpp).
>
> Can you control this kind of thing with the t0 parameter?
>
> I found a setting that works better for this model:
>
>
> $ ./fhrb sample num_samples=50 num_warmup=50 algorithm=hmc engine=nuts stepsize=1 data file=fhrb.data.R output refresh=1
>
> 50 iterations are enough to warm up so the algorithm converges to
> the right answer.  The tree depth is still 7, though, which is higher than
> the 3 we get with Stan 1.3.   If you run same setting with more warmup, things
> seem to be worse.
>
> But I noticed that the step size reported by bin/print didn't vary between 0.1 and 1.9 like I expected.
> Is it only an initial value?   The initial value doesn't seem to matter much because it gets
> cut down very quickly.

Did you mean to specify a non-zero step size jitter parameter?

More warm-up causing problems is not a good sign -- it's still possible that 1.3 was missing a region of significant probability mass
with high curvature.

If you wanted to tweak the dual averaging you want to play with the kappa parameter.  I just did the derivation but I left it at work, but if I remember correctly
then if your chain has autocorrelation length N (in number of iterations, not actual integration time) then you want to take kappa to be

kappa = 1.0 / log_{10} (1 + N).

