[For a casual reader: this is not with the original multivariate model of Fernando, but with a different model with less dramatic slowness. Michael and Bob, I don't know if this is relevant to the problem of the original model any more, but I continue reporting because I started.]

I did some more testing related to rejection periods shown by trace plots. The problem seems to be two scale-like parameters of a prior structure, trend_sigma and trend2_sigma, one of which goes near zero, and then the sampler starts to reject. The priors of these parameters were improper; I changed them to be weakly informative (half-cauchy, lognormal, gamma, ...) but that did not help. Setting a proper vague prior for all parameters of the structure maybe helps, but does not entirely remove the problem. At least the priors and changing a cauchy to a gaussian speed up sampling.

Then I switched back to 1.3 again, and tested with the improved, speedier model: Stan 2.0 about 100 seconds, v. 1.3 about 34-40 seconds.
Average tree depth seems to be a bit higher with 2.0.

I don't quite understand how the hyper(hyper)priors help, if they do, for I don't see any pathology with the geometry. (But my experience is limited.) I even fail to see why the sampler gets stuck with small values of the scales. (That does make the whole model to fit less well, but not dramatically so. I know the classic funnel but these scales are higher on the hierarchy and  do not affect likelihood so much.)

Right now I'm tired of switching between 1.3 and 2.0, and have other things to do. My model is not critical in any way, it is just an experiment as I wanted to get more familiar with Stan. The hyperparameter structure that causes problems could actually be removed without much effect.

Overall I think Stan is a great project. It has potential to revolutionize certain kind of applied statistical or machine learning work.

On Saturday, October 19, 2013 10:39:16 AM UTC+3, Janne Sinkkonen wrote:

> The speed difference between 1.3 and 2.0 seems to be a bit less than I previously indicated, I had made model updates that slow it down. For my model it's more like 20-25 secs vs 40-60 secs. Sorry for the wrong information earlier.

> [...]

> Traceplots look good but have a couple of periods of stuckiness across all parameters.

