On 10/19/13 11:12 AM, Janne Sinkkonen wrote:
> [For a casual reader: this is not with the original multivariate model of Fernando, but with a different model with less
> dramatic slowness. Michael and Bob, I don't know if this is relevant to the problem of the original model any more, but
> I continue reporting because I started.]
>
> I did some more testing related to rejection periods shown by trace plots. The problem seems to be two scale-like
> parameters of a prior structure, trend_sigma and trend2_sigma, one of which goes near zero, and then the sampler starts
> to reject. The priors of these parameters were improper; I changed them to be weakly informative (half-cauchy,
> lognormal, gamma, ...) but that did not help. Setting a proper vague prior for all parameters of the structure maybe
> helps, but does not entirely remove the problem. At least the priors and changing a cauchy to a gaussian speed up sampling.

This is the usual suspect.  It sounds like you're familar with
Neal's funnel example (described in the manual).

> Then I switched back to 1.3 again, and tested with the improved, speedier model: Stan 2.0 about 100 seconds, v. 1.3
> about 34-40 seconds.
> Average tree depth seems to be a bit higher with 2.0.

What's the effective sample/second rate?

> I don't quite understand how the hyper(hyper)priors help, if they do, for I don't see any pathology with the geometry.

It makes the tails less fat.  So it's actually helping
in the other direction, keeping values from wandering
away to +infinity.

The problem with the geometry is again made clear by
Radford's funnel example.

> (But my experience is limited.) I even fail to see why the sampler gets stuck with small values of the scales.

Lower scale restricts the movements of lower-level parameters.

This is why we've been recommending the "Matt trick" non-centered
reparameterization.  Also discussed in the same section of the manual.

> (That
> does make the whole model to fit less well, but not dramatically so. I know the classic funnel but these scales are
> higher on the hierarchy and  do not affect likelihood so much.)

There's the effect on likelihood and the effect of restricting
the lower-level parameters.

> Right now I'm tired of switching between 1.3 and 2.0, and have other things to do. My model is not critical in any way,
> it is just an experiment as I wanted to get more familiar with Stan. The hyperparameter structure that causes problems
> could actually be removed without much effect.

Thanks for all the help!

> Overall I think Stan is a great project. It has potential to revolutionize certain kind of applied statistical or
> machine learning work.

Thanks for the encouragement, too.  We'll get this sorted out and
we'll report back to the list --- I think we just got a little over-eager
in releasing 2.0.

- Bob

