I estimated a model that had both cluster and individual specific
variability (still with the poisson, as opposed to invidual
variability via the negative binomial); it worked extremely poorly for
reasons I have yet to diagnose.  I expected some trouble identifying
individual vs cluster effects, but not that much!  There are some
relatively large clusters (~ 100 members), and I expected that was
enough for identification.

>>
>> Any advice on choosing the negative binomial vs poisson with random
>> intercepts?
>
> The negative binomial IS a Poisson mixture model:

I meant as a matter of efficiency, which you answered below.  Of
course, whatever was screwing up my explicit mixture model may affect
teh negative binomial as well.

Foonote: my naive specification of the mixture used a normal
distribution for log(lambda), which isn't quite the gamma distribution
that produces the negative binomial in conjunction with the poisson.

>
> http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Negative_binomial_distribution#Gamma.E2.80.93Poisson_mixture
>
> Using negative binomial should be more efficient because it
> integrates out the intermediate gamma-distributed mean parameter
> analytically.

OK.  After the remark that I might have better luck specifying the
student-t as an explicit mixture model, I though similar logic might
apply to the negative binomial.

