> When you speak of the dispersion of sigma_beta growing (or did you mean
> the size of sigma_beta itself?), do you mean growing as the acceptance
> rate increases?

You should see the variance/intervals of sigma_beta grow as you increase the target acceptance probability.

> It's a bit odd: the percentiles haven't changed much, but visually the
> sigma_beta chains seem to be making more frequent excursions into the
> higher values.
>

> Does increasing the acceptance rate effectively reduce the step size?
> I'm trying to understand why it would be helpful with heavy tails.

Exactly -- you're really decreasing the step size and allowing easier transitions into the tails.

> For sigma_beta that's about x 24 effective samples/second by pushing adapt_delta up to 0.9.

Be careful, since you can't always trust ESS calculations when the step size is too large.  To be confident in your
settings you want to generate something like the attached figure and really see the intervals for latent parameters
like sigma_beta stabilize,

